  (18728,'/yandex-google-seo-leak','yandex-google-seo-leak','post','blog','How the Yandex Leak Will Forever Change Your SEO','How the Yandex Leak Will Forever Change Your SEO - Alpha Efficiency','In this article, we''ll show you interesting discoveries from Yandex data leak that can change your SEO game forever.','https://alphaefficiency.com/yandex-google-seo-leak',1,'Allegedly, a former employee has leaked Yandex&#8217;s code fragments. This revealed nearly 2,000 search ranking factors the Russian technology company uses for forming results pages. And today, our SEO agency in Chicago will show you interesting discoveries from the Yandex leak that can forever change your SEO game. Hold on tight, as the importance of some &hellip; Continued','<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Allegedly, a former employee has leaked Yandex&#8217;s code fragments. This revealed nearly 2,000 search ranking factors the Russian technology company uses for forming results pages. And today, our </span><a class="editor-rtfLink" href="/seo-agency-chicago" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span data-preserver-spaces="true">SEO agency in Chicago</span></a><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> will show you interesting discoveries from the Yandex leak that can forever change your SEO game. Hold on tight, as the importance of some search ranking factors might surprise you.</span></p>
<h5>Table of Contents:</h5>
<ul>
<li><a href="/#yandex-google-similarities">Yandex and Google Are More Similar Than You May Believe</a></li>
<li><a href="/#ranking-factors-revealed">1,922 SEO Ranking Factors Revealed &#8211; Here Are Key Insights</a>
<ul>
<li><a href="/#backlink-age">Backlink Age is a Ranking Factor (+ More Link-Related Discoveries)</a></li>
<li><a href="/#ppc">Buying PPC Affects Rankings &#8211; The Percentage of Organic Traffic Matters</a></li>
<li><a href="/#on-page-advertising">Avoid On-Page Advertising If You Want To Rank High</a></li>
<li><a href="/#content">Yandex Prefers Older Content</a></li>
<li><a href="/#main-page">Keep Your Important Pages Close To Main Page &#8211; Here&#8217;s Why&#8230;</a></li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><a href="/#seo-change">How Can Insights from Yandex Code Leak Change Your SEO Forever?</a></li>
</ul>
<img src="https://alphaefficiency.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/02/Yandex-code-leaks.png" alt="The importance of Yandex code leaks for SEO" width="800" height="600" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
<h2 id="yandex-google-similarities"><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Yandex and Google Are More Similar Than You May Believe</span></h2>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Both Yandex and Google are separate search engines and big tech firms that constantly push the limits of technology. But before we start exploring the learnings we&#8217;ve got from the leakage of Yandex&#8217;s source code repositories, please note that this won&#8217;t show us the exact way Google&#8217;s algorithms work. Don&#8217;t blindly follow the search ranking factors we&#8217;re going to reveal. However, these two search engines might be more similar than people often believe. Let&#8217;s see why:</span></p>
<ul>
<li><strong><span data-preserver-spaces="true">MatrixNet and RankBrain are not the same, but&#8230;</span></strong></li>
</ul>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">In 2009, Yandex introduced MatrixNet &#8211; a neural network many people believe is similar to Google&#8217;s RankBrain. However, this isn&#8217;t completely true. As a limited algorithm, RankBrain focuses on understanding 15% of search queries no one has used on Google before. On the other hand, MatrixNet does much more. It&#8217;s a machine learning model that classifies each search query in order to apply the appropriate search ranking algorithms. This makes ranking factors dynamic, as different factors may be applied for each search query, depending on its class.</span></p>
<ul>
<li><strong><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Engineers from Yandex and Google share findings at conferences</span></strong></li>
</ul>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Engineers from both Yandex and Google often attend the same conferences and share their findings with each other. The technology behind search engines is constantly evolving, and both companies are always looking for ways to improve their search results and make their user experiences better. By sharing their ideas and developments in machine learning, information retrieval, and natural language processing fields, both big tech firms can benefit. </span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Also, by doing research on LinkedIn, you can find hundreds of engineers who have worked for both Yandex and Google. All of this leads to the conclusion that, while not the same, algorithms both search engines use do have some similarities. </span></p>
<h2 id="ranking-factors-revealed"><span data-preserver-spaces="true">1,922 SEO Ranking Factors Revealed &#8211; Here are Key Insights</span></h2>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">A Yandex source code repository leak is definitely one of the biggest events in the SEO sphere in years. It revealed 1,922 search ranking factors this big tech company is using, at least as of July 2022. However, keep in mind that there are many other factors that haven&#8217;t been revealed in this Yandex code leak. By analyzing breach contents, we&#8217;ve found out that there are 17,854 ranking factors in total. </span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">To help you make a better sense of all the factors revealed in Yandex leaks, we&#8217;ve put up the list of most important discoveries:</span></p>
<h3 id="backlink-age"><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Backlink Age is a Ranking Factor (+ More Link-Related Discoveries)</span></h3>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Since the Nepot filter in 2005, Yandex has been battling link manipulation in a similar way that Google does. One of the ways the Russian tech company does this is by including backlink age as one of the search ranking factors. Older backlinks carry more weight and can improve your site&#8217;s ranking in search results. This means that investing in high-quality backlinks will pay off in the future as they start aging.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Here are more link-related search ranking factors that you should keep in mind:</span></p>
<ul>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Links from the top 100 websites by PageRank can have an impact on your rankings</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">The topic similarity between two websites plays a role in determining link relevance</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Link quality affects link relevance</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Backlinks from homepages are more important than those from internal pages</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">The percentage of query words in links matter</span></li>
</ul>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">With this in mind, it&#8217;s a good idea to perform the </span><a class="editor-rtfLink" href="/backlink-analysis-101" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span data-preserver-spaces="true">backlink analysis</span></a><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> in order to get a better insight into your own and your competitor&#8217;s backlink quality.</span></p>
<h3 id="ppc"><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Buying PPC affects Rankings &#8211; The Percentage of Organic Traffic Matters</span></h3>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">We&#8217;ve found out that the percentage of organic traffic is a ranking factor on Yandex. This means that obtaining traffic through PPC advertising can have a negative impact on your rankings in this popular search engine. Yandex leaks also confirm that direct traffic (visits to your website that don&#8217;t have a defined source) have a positive impact on rankings. Here are some interesting ranking factors you can find in Yandex source code files:</span></p>
<ul>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Percentage of direct visits when complete traffic is calculated</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">The number of unique visitors</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Green traffic share (direct visits) from desktop devices</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Green traffic share (direct visits) from mobile devices</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Search traffic &#8211; the percentage of transitions from search engines to your site</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Share of visits that don&#8217;t come from links</span></li>
</ul>
<h3 id="on-page-advertising"><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Avoid On-Page Advertising If You Want To Rank High</span></h3>
<p><strong><span data-preserver-spaces="true">FI_ADV:</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> -0.2509284637 &#8211; this factor indicates that any type of advertising on your page can make it not rank high in the Yandex search engine. </span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">And there&#8217;s also another factor that calculates the average ratio of adverts on the screen. It&#8217;s not fully clear what exactly this means &#8211; but we assume that showing too many ads on users&#8217; screens will negatively impact your rankings. Google has similar mechanisms to penalize you if your obtrusive ads are ruining the user experience. So be careful.</span></p>
<h3 id="content"><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Yandex prefers older content</span></h3>
<p><strong><span data-preserver-spaces="true">FI_DATER_AGE:</span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> -0.2074373667 &#8211; The popular search engine uses a dater function to determine how old your document is. The value will be 1 if you&#8217;ve published a piece of content today and 0 if it&#8217;s 10 years or older. This makes it clear that Yandex prefers older content.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Considering this ranking factor, we advise you to update an older content piece whenever possible instead of publishing new ones on the same topic.</span></p>
<h3 id="main-page"><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Keep Your Important Pages Close to Main Page &#8211; Here&#8217;s Why&#8230;</span></h3>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Yandex leaks revealed code fragments that indicate this search engine&#8217;s preference for pages that are closer to the homepage. Although crawl depth is not an explicit ranking factor for Google, it&#8217;s still possible that the most popular search engine gives at least a small advantage to pages that can be reached within one click from the homepage. Google might be using crawl depth as a way to determine which pages are the most important on your website.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">As it&#8217;s always better to be safe than sorry, we advise you to keep your important pages closer to the homepage. This means one click from the main page for your top pages and less than three clicks for important ones.</span></p>
<h2 id="seo-change"><span data-preserver-spaces="true">How Can Insights from Yandex Code Leak Change Your SEO Forever?</span></h2>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">It&#8217;s just natural to wonder to which extent are Yandex&#8217;s search ranking factors similar to Google&#8217;s. Yet, only software engineers from Google might be able to give an accurate answer to that. It&#8217;s not worth obsessing over that question. After all, these two big tech firms constantly work on new technology, and some of these ranking factors may become irrelevant as new ones arise in the future.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Instead, try to observe Yandex leaks as a chance to get deeper insights into the way modern search engines function. Until this huge event, the search marketing community has been relying on early findings from the 2000s way too much. Search engine algorithms have rapidly evolved since then, and now you have an opportunity to catch up with changes and adjust your SEO strategies for future success.</span></p>','https://alphaefficiency.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/02/Yandex-code-leaks.webp',NULL,'[]','[344]','2023-02-01T12:22:06','2023-02-01T12:22:06',1297,2,1),
  (18741,'/auto-parts-ecommerce-website-development','auto-parts-ecommerce-website-development','post','blog','Auto parts eCommerce website development process','Auto parts eCommerce website development process - Alpha Efficiency','In this article, our agency will give you a complete insight into the successful auto parts eCommerce website development process.','https://alphaefficiency.com/auto-parts-ecommerce-website-development',1,'If you own an auto parts business, you probably know how difficult it can be to keep up with the competition during the digital age. As more and more people order auto parts online nowadays, in order to thrive in the competitive auto parts industry market &#8211; besides offering the best possible prices and having &hellip; Continued','<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">If you own an auto parts business, you probably know how difficult it can be to keep up with the competition during the digital age. As more and more people order auto parts online nowadays, in order to thrive in the competitive auto parts industry market &#8211; besides offering the best possible prices and having the latest and greatest products in your store, you also need to have a visually appealing and functional website that will make your products available 24/7 for potential customers. Auto parts eCommerce website development is a complex task, and if you opt for the DIY approach and use auto parts website builders, you might quickly get stuck. </span></p>
