<li><a href="/#adobe-flash-animations"><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Outdated Trend #7: Adobe Flash Animations</span></a></li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><a href="/#profitable-aesthetics"><span data-preserver-spaces="true">How To Increase Your Conversion Rates In 7 Days (Without Expensive Redesigns)</span></a></li>
</ul>
<img src="https://alphaefficiency.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/outdated-web-design-trends.png" alt="outdated web design trends" width="800" height="600" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
<h2 id="victims"><span data-preserver-spaces="true">How Businesses Become Victims Of Outdated Web Design Trends</span></h2>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">First, let&#8217;s discuss why so many businesses become victims of outdated web design trends. This will give you a general idea of recognizing practices that are more likely to stick for years.</span></p>
<h3><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Ignoring to improve a website that &#8220;works&#8221; on time</span></h3>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">It&#8217;s easy to become comfortable with a website that &#8220;works&#8221; and neglect the chance to make it &#8220;work&#8221; even better.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">If you designed your website a few years ago (even if you hired an agency back then), chances are that it </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">has outdated</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> elements.</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> Recognizing them is the first step to increasing profitability.</span></p>
<h3><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Hiring freelancers with outdated skills</span></h3>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Are you planning to hire freelancers to build or maintain your website?</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">This can be cost-effective, but&#8230;</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Many freelancers (not everyone) rely on skills and best practices they learned during a course they bought five years ago. Yet, things change quickly in the web design industry and you need daily education.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Moreover, freelance web designers rarely have well-rounded skills to build profitable digital assets, meaning even if they nail the aesthetics, your website functionality or SEO strategies might become questionable.</span></p>
<h3><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Over-reliance on DIY platforms</span></h3>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">With no-code website builders, creating digital assets has </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">never</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> been </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">easier</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">.</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> At first look, you can create </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">amazing</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> digital assets without any design or coding knowledge using Elementor, Wix, and similar platforms.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">The problem is&#8230;</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Templates you&#8217;ll find on such platforms </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">are</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> usually </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">outdated</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> before you even get to launch your website.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Unfortunately, most users lack the technical skills to customize these templates. As a result, they settle for a design that:</span></p>
<ul>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Doesn&#8217;t 100% match their vision.</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Looks &#8216;okay&#8217; but </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">not </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">amazing</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">.</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Suffers in terms of performance.</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Is set to become outdated within a year.</span></li>
</ul>
<h3><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Resistance to change</span></h3>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Change can be intimidating, and many business owners resist it, fearing that a redesign might disrupt their operations or alienate existing customers.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">A familiar but outdated design will eventually hurt your revenue, making you wonder, &#8216;</span><a class="editor-rtfLink" href="/why-is-my-conversion-rate-so-low" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Why is my conversion rate so low?</span></a><span data-preserver-spaces="true">&#8216;</span></p>
<h2 id="consequences"><span data-preserver-spaces="true">What Will Happen If You Don&#8217;t Ditch Outdated Website Design Trends?</span></h2>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Failing to keep your web design up-to-date</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> usually results in </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">serious</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> consequences. You might not notice all of these immediately, but analyzing your website&#8217;s performance over </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">longer</span> <span data-preserver-spaces="true">periods of time</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> will reveal the following:</span></p>
<ul>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Decline in user engagement levels</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Reduced conversion rates</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Poor mobile experience</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Negative impact on SEO</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Eroded brand image</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Increased maintenance costs</span></li>
</ul>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">You&#8217;d be surprised how quick the downfall from the &#8216;state of the art&#8217; to the outdated web design is.</span></p>
<h2 id="7-outdated-trends"><span data-preserver-spaces="true">7 Outdated Web Design Trends In 2024 (And What To Use Instead)</span></h2>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">So, what are the outdated web design trends you should pay close attention to this year? Let&#8217;s find out&#8230;</span></p>
<h3 id="stock-photos"><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Outdated Trend #1: Using Stock Photos Is Seen As Low-Effort</span></h3>
<img src="https://alphaefficiency.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/Using-stock-photos-is-an-outdated-web-design-trend.png" alt="Using stock photos is an outdated web design trend" width="800" height="600" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">If the first thing you see when you open a website </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">are</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> generic stock images, </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">chances are you</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> won&#8217;t be impressed.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Stock photos send low-effort vibes. </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Some potential customers </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">might</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> even </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">start to</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> wonder if your business is legitimate.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Stock images were popular back when creating custom photography was expensive and </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">difficult</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">. In 2024, they&#8217;re among the top outdated design trends and a big &#8216;no&#8217; in </span><a class="editor-rtfLink" href="/web-design-branding" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span data-preserver-spaces="true">web design branding</span></a><span data-preserver-spaces="true">.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Today, you don&#8217;t even need to hire a professional photographer to create amazing photos for your website. All you need is a solid phone camera and a spark of creativity.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">And once</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> you replace stock photos on your website with genuine photos of your team or office, you&#8217;ll notice a big difference</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">&#8230;</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Let&#8217;s say you&#8217;re creating a fitness web design. If you use stock photos of the gym and staff members, people will get confused once they arrive at your </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">physical</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> location. </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">But if you take actual photos of your gym and fitness instructors, they&#8217;d be happy to see familiar faces </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">once they arrive</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">The trust-building process becomes much easier once you ditch stock photography, among all other benefits.</span></p>
<h3 id="aggressive-popups"><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Outdated Trend #2: Aggressive Popups &#8211; Mobile Users HATE Them</span></h3>
<img src="https://alphaefficiency.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/Aggressive-popups-on-mobile-website-version-is-an-outdated-trend.png" alt="Aggressive popups on mobile website version is an outdated trend" width="800" height="600" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Did this ever happen to you?</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">You&#8217;re lying on the couch, exploring the offer that caught your eye while your meal is warming up.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">The website looks trustworthy. It&#8217;s intuitive, and you enjoy scrolling through it.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">You&#8217;re</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> almost ready to buy; you just want to learn about the final details&#8230; You quickly find the information you were looking for and start reading&#8230;</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Ding!</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">An annoying popup appears.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Okay, you&#8217;ll click that little cross on the top right corner </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">as usual</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">.</span> <span data-preserver-spaces="true">The button is small, but you </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">somehow </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">manage to</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> close the popup with your thumb.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Let&#8217;s continue &#8211; where was that thing you were looking for? Oh, there it is&#8230;</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">A minute later &#8211; another popup.</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> The blood pressure is rising&#8230;</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">But this time, you hear two dings. One is coming from your microwave &#8211; the meal is ready.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">You close the website and put the phone down out of frustration.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">This</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> is </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">exactly</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> why aggressive popups are among the top outdated web design trends to avoid in 2024 (and forever).</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">In less than five minutes, your positive first impression of a website turned into a horror user experience.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">While popups </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">do</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> have their place in web design and marketing, they </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">need to</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> be subtle.</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> It&#8217;s rarely a good idea to include them on your website. If a popup doesn&#8217;t offer immediate value to the user (or warn them about the urgent action they must take) &#8211; do not use it.</span></p>
<p><strong><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Bonus tip: </span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces="true">I highly recommend setting triggers for your popups. </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">This way, they&#8217;ll only appear after the potential customer takes specific action, ensuring they </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">don&#8217;t</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> get </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">interrupted</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> at the </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">wrong</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> time.</span></p>
<h3 id="desktop-hamburger"><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Outdated Trend #3: Hamburger Menus On Desktop Versions Is A Web Design Sin In 2024</span></h3>
<img src="https://alphaefficiency.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/Hamburger-menus-on-desktop.png" alt="Hamburger menus on desktop" width="800" height="600" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Hamburger menus, those three-line icons that hide navigation options until clicked, have become a best practice in mobile design. They save space and look good on smaller screens.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">However, carrying this outdated website design trend </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">over</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> to desktop versions is a different story.</span></p>
<ul>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Desktop users have the luxury of space.</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Adding hamburger menus adds an unnecessary step to the user experience.</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Hamburger menus on desktop versions make it harder for users to discover all the available options on your site.</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">They lead to frustration and higher bounce rates.</span></li>
</ul>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">What can you do instead?</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">The first option is to use the traditional top navigation bar. It&#8217;s a tried-and-true method with which internet users are familiar.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">However, if your website has </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">a lot of</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> categories or subcategories, using mega menus might be a smart web design decision.</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> This type of menu expands to show multiple options in a well-organized layout, making it easy for users to find what they&#8217;re looking for without feeling overwhelmed. </span></p>
<h3 id="carousels"><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Outdated Trend #4: Auto-Play Carousels Are Not That Impressive Anymore (And Will Ruin Your Conversion Rates)</span></h3>
<img src="https://alphaefficiency.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/Auto-Play-carousels-are-website-trend-to-avoid-in-2024.png" alt="Auto-Play carousels are website trend to avoid in 2024" width="800" height="600" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">When it was first possible to set carousels on auto-play</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">, it was impressive</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">.</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> Many thought it was a great way to show multiple offers at once.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">However, as web design has evolved, it has become clear that auto-play carousels are more of a hindrance than a help.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Auto-play carousels switch content automatically, often before users have finished reading the current slide. </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">This</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> can be frustrating and disruptive, leading to a poor user experience.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">While some people still love the dynamic look of auto-play carousels, including them in your website project is a high-risk, low-reward strategy. Here&#8217;s why:</span></p>
<ul>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">User disruption can lead to higher bounce rates.</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Presenting too many offers or messages at once can overwhelm users. </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Instead of absorbing the information, they may </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">end up ignoring</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> it altogether.</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Each image or slide adds to the overall load time. The trade-off between flashy carousels and fast-loading pages is rarely worth it.</span></li>
</ul>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Instead of auto-play carousels, I recommend using static hero images or manual carousels.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Another </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">interesting</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> solution for your website might be creating a </span><a class="editor-rtfLink" href="/bento-box-design" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span data-preserver-spaces="true">bento box website design</span></a><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> to highlight </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">key</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> offers or messages in separate, static sections on your homepage.</span></p>
<h3 id="flat-design"><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Outdated Trend #5: Flat Design Is Starting To Look Boring To Users</span></h3>
<img src="https://alphaefficiency.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/Flat-Design-is-outdated.png" alt="Flat Design is outdated" width="800" height="600" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Does your website design look flatter than a pancake? Does it lack the tactile feel </span><a class="editor-rtfLink" href="/modern-ui-design" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span data-preserver-spaces="true">modern UI designs</span></a><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> are known for?</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Chances are, it</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> is starting to look outdated.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Flat design </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">was once celebrated</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> for its simplicity and minimalism. It stripped away the skeuomorphic elements that mimicked real-world objects and focused on clean, two-dimensional aesthetics.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">While this trend brought a fresh, modern look </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">to websites</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">, its overuse has led to a backlash.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Flat design can now appear lifeless and uninspiring. Internet users today crave more engaging and interactive experiences.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Many </span><a class="editor-rtfLink" href="/graphic-design-trends-2024" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span data-preserver-spaces="true">graphic design trends </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">in</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> 2024</span></a><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> revolve around offering a sense of touch and interaction, which flat design inherently </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">lacks</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">There are many options to improve your designs (you can take a subtle route too):</span></p>
<ul>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Add three-dimensional fonts.</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Use gradients to add visual interest.</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Create </span><a class="editor-rtfLink" href="/glassmorphism-ui" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span data-preserver-spaces="true">glassmorphism UI designs</span></a><span data-preserver-spaces="true">.</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">And much more.</span></li>
</ul>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Many fear that adding a dimension to their website might </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">lead to</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> accessibility issues. But with modern techniques, </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">it&#8217;s</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> rarely the case.</span></p>
<h3 id="static-pages"><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Outdated Trend #6: Completely Static Pages Scream That They Were Built 10 Years Ago</span></h3>
<img src="https://alphaefficiency.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/Static-Pages-outdated-web-design-trends.png" alt="Static Pages - outdated web design trends" width="800" height="600" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Similar to flat design, static pages that lack </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">any</span> <a class="editor-rtfLink" href="/ui-animation" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span data-preserver-spaces="true">UI animation</span></a><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> or hover effects have been outdated for years </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">already</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">The main reason why modern</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> users hate static pages</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">? Because such pages</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> don&#8217;t provide any interaction or feedback.</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> </span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Modern web users are accustomed to fluid transitions, animated elements, and responsive interactions that guide their experience and keep them engaged. </span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">There are many </span><a class="editor-rtfLink" href="/web-page-effects" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span data-preserver-spaces="true">web page effects</span></a><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> that </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">might</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> suit your branding and bring your site to life.</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> It&#8217;s time to ditch static pages </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">along with</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> other outdated web design trends.</span></p>
<h3 id="adobe-flash-animations"><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Outdated Trend #7: Adobe Flash Animations</span></h3>
<img src="https://alphaefficiency.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/Adobe-Flash-Animations-are-outdated-in-2024.png" alt="Adobe Flash Animations are outdated in 2024" width="800" height="600" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Adobe Flash animations were once a hallmark of cutting-edge web design. They allowed for rich, interactive experiences that captivated users and set websites apart </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">from the competition</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">However, as technology and web standards have evolved, Flash has become outdated and is now considered a significant liability for modern websites.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">The decline of Flash</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> began with its incompatibility with mobile devices. Apple&#8217;s decision not to support Flash on iOS devices marked the beginning of the end. Over time, data security vulnerabilities, performance issues, and the rise of HTML5 led to Flash </span><span data-preserver-spaces="true">being</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> phased out.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">In 2020, Adobe officially ended support for Flash Player, signaling the definitive end of its era.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">To bring your website up to modern standards,</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> consider switching to HTML to create interactive and animated web content.</span><span data-preserver-spaces="true"> It is widely supported across all devices and browsers, offering a secure and efficient way to deliver rich user experiences.</span></p>
