<img src="https://alphaefficiency.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/11/small-business-tools.png" alt="small business tools" width="800" height="600" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
<h2 id="software-choice"><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Why Software Choice Matters For Small Businesses?</span></h2>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Creating a powerful tool stack for your small business can make a difference in many areas. The right combination of business apps can help you:</span></p>
<ul>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Improve employee productivity</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Strengthen your hiring process and find the best candidates</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Make team communication more productive and enjoyable</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Make project management easier</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Make money management more effective</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Reach your target audience with better accuracy</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Maintain client satisfaction</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Reduce manual workload and streamline your marketing efforts</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">and more</span></li>
</ul>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">You and your team will use these small business tools daily. Their interfaces will become your digital house where you&#8217;ll do most of your work-related activities. And just like you wouldn&#8217;t buy a house you don&#8217;t truly like, why would you pay for small business software neither you nor your employees enjoy working with?</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">So, if you&#8217;re a small business owner, I recommend consulting with your employees when building your tool stack. Also, don&#8217;t always settle for the cheapest options. Empowering your team with the right tools will make them more productive. Investing in proper tools pays off in both the short and long run.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">And if you&#8217;re working 9-5 and you&#8217;re frustrated by the software your company is using, don&#8217;t be afraid to explain your concerns to the boss. <a href="/software-choice">Software choice</a> matters, especially for businesses operating on tight budgets and unlimited dreams.</span></p>
<h2 id="how-to-choose"><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Don&#8217;t Choose Your Small Business Tools Before Answering These 6 Key Questions</span></h2>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">As a tech geek, I usually want to try out every app I hear someone praise. I spent a lot of money on tools that didn&#8217;t do me or my business much good. And those were not necessarily bad tools &#8211; they had many positive reviews and brought profit to many other small businesses. However, these software solutions were simply not a good fit for me and my digital agency.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Yes, choosing the right software for your small business can be challenging. I know that the list of 100 small business tools you&#8217;ve come here for won&#8217;t help much with decision paralysis. I could have made this a list of the ten best tools and called it a day. But there are probably bigger chances of me winning a lottery than shortlisting the exact ten tools that best fit your small business. </span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Before we move on, I want you to understand that not all of the 100 small business software you&#8217;ll find in this article truly suit your company. Being aware of this can save you thousands of dollars. It can save you months of work, too.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Luckily, there is a way to quickly narrow down the choice and find tools that can actually drive growth for your small business. Whenever you&#8217;re tempted to try a new software, start by answering these six questions:</span></p>
<ol>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Does it fit the rest of my business ecosystem?</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Does it solve my actual problems?</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Is it easy to use for humans?</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Is it mobile-friendly?</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Does it suit my budget?</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Does it come with extensive Support?</span></li>
</ol>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Let&#8217;s elaborate on these questions&#8230;</span></p>
<h3><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Does it fit the rest of my business ecosystem?</span></h3>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">When considering a new tool, analyze how it would fit with other apps you currently use for your business.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">If it supports integrations with all your current tools, you shouldn&#8217;t have any problem implementing it in your ecosystem. You&#8217;ll be able to find a combination of tools that will help you with business development.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">However, if it&#8217;s impossible to implement a tool in your existing ecosystem, you should avoid it. Otherwise, syncing your data and ensuring a smooth workflow might become challenging down the road. After all, there are dozens of great tools for each need, so keep looking.</span></p>
<h3><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Does it solve my actual problems?</span></h3>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Try to evaluate all the gaps in your current system and see if the small business tool you&#8217;re considering is filling any of those. You don&#8217;t want to pay for a software solution that doesn&#8217;t solve any problems you&#8217;re currently facing or that completely overlaps with another tool serving you well.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">As a software enthusiast, I used to spend a lot of money on apps that promised to solve problems that I didn&#8217;t really face. If I could roll back time, I&#8217;d invest that portion of my budget in other essential business aspects.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">And what if you&#8217;re unsure whether you have a problem or think a specific tool could improve your workflow? In that case, I recommend free trials or paying a one-month subscription. You want to delay getting an annual deal until you know it&#8217;s the best possible option among other small business tools.</span></p>
<h3><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Is it easy to use for humans?</span></h3>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Don&#8217;t let the shiny features and advanced functionalities trick you into paying for a tool you don&#8217;t love using. Sometimes, you&#8217;ll get better results with simpler tools designed for your knowledge level.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Another important consideration is how intuitive the interface is. For example, if you need to open too many tabs to get things done, it might disrupt your rhythm and prevent you from achieving deep focus. Not to mention that using such unintuitive tools feels like hell on smartphones.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Before committing to any software and integrating it into my business ecosystem, I encourage my employees to give me honest opinions. I encourage them to test various features and see if they enjoy getting things done with this new tool. </span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Again, if there&#8217;s a free trial, I usually take advantage of it &#8211; but if there isn&#8217;t any, I don&#8217;t mind subscribing for a month to see if it solves any problems and how enjoyable it is for my people. If my team doesn&#8217;t like it, I check other options. Don&#8217;t let the shiny features trick you into paying for a tool you don&#8217;t love using.</span></p>
<h3><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Is it mobile-friendly? </span></h3>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Whenever evaluating an application, I check if it&#8217;s easy to use via smartphone. If the tool is not mobile-friendly, I tend to avoid it. This is because I often run Alpha Efficiency on the move. But even if you&#8217;re working on a desktop most of the time, there will be cases when you need to perform some tasks quickly. If the software doesn&#8217;t allow you to do this smoothly while waiting for your breakfast in the restaurant, you might find yourself in trouble one day.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">So, if you&#8217;re in a dilemma between two similar tools, one mobile-friendly and the other not, you shouldn&#8217;t think twice before choosing a mobile-friendly one.</span></p>
<h3><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Does it suit my budget?</span></h3>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">If you&#8217;re working with a tight budget, prioritize tools that solve the most burning questions. Small business resources are often limited, so you must spend wisely.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">You can also go with free options. But be aware that free tools come with limitations. Usually, investing heavily in top-tier software pays off, especially if that software is closely related to your core business operations. Again, prioritize your most burning problems.</span></p>
<h3><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Does it come with extensive Support?</span></h3>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">The final question is whether the small business tools you&#8217;re considering come with good customer support. There might be cases in the future where you&#8217;ll run into problems or need help setting up the software properly. If the company behind a tool invests in customer support, you&#8217;ll be able to solve most problems within minutes. And just imagine how much profit you can lose if you have to wait for days before your concerns get addressed.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Now, this is not a definite rule &#8211; but more expensive tools tend to come with better customer support. While most companies tend to claim that they provide excellent support, sometimes, this is only the case for their premium plans. I recommend checking customer reviews to find a realistic answer to this question.</span></p>
<h2>90 Small Business Tools That Can Help You Generate Six-Figure Months</h2>
