<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Notion is cost-efficient, and it provides excellent value for money.</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">It is enjoyable to use on mobile devices thanks to a dedicated app.</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">You&#8217;ll find a wide range of free templates that can help you speed up the setup process.</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">You can save your AI prompts for reuse.</span></li>
</ul>
<p><strong><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Cons:</span></strong></p>
<ul>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Lack of goal tracking means you might need to combine other project management tools for small businesses during large projects.</span></li>
</ul>
<p><strong><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Price: </span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Free plan; $8 per month for the Pro plan; $15 per month for the Business plan; </span></p>
<h4>25. Asana</h4>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Asana stands out as an agile project management software suitable for businesses of all sizes. If you&#8217;re looking for a tool with a simple and intuitive interface, I recommend giving Asana a chance. While it might have its limitations, its core offerings and ease of use make it a solid choice for companies with a limited budget. One feature I particularly liked within this project management app was the Workflow Builder, a beneficial tool to automate repetitive tasks that consume a lot of time.</span></p>
<p><strong><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Key features:</span></strong></p>
