</ul>
<p><strong><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Pros:</span></strong></p>
<ul>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">A free plan makes it accessible for small teams and startups.</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">It integrates smoothly with a wide range of 3rd party tools.</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Collaboration-focused</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">I loved 2-factor authentication, which keeps your business data safe.</span></li>
</ul>
<p><strong><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Cons:</span></strong></p>
<ul>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Limited time tracking</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">While Asana provides export options, they are limited to CSV and JSON formats, which might not be ideal for all users.</span></li>
<li><span data-preserver-spaces="true">A bit of a jump between pricing tiers</span></li>
</ul>
<p><strong><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Price: </span></strong><span data-preserver-spaces="true">The Basic plan is free; the Premium plan costs $10.99 per month; the Business plan costs $24.99 per month. </span></p>
<h4>26. Trello</h4>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Even in a landscape crowded with numerous project management tools, Trello remains a relevant choice. During the early stages of my agency, Trello was my go-to platform for its simplicity and efficiency in organizing tasks. However, as the demands of my projects expanded and my team grew, I found the need to transition to more robust project management tools.</span></p>
<p><span data-preserver-spaces="true">Trello has a limited set of features, which doesn&#8217;t mean it&#8217;s a worse choice than other project management tools. It heavily depends on your needs. If your business is dealing with simple projects and you want to easily keep track of simple tasks, you won&#8217;t find many better small business tools than Trello.</span></p>
